"""Build the valuation workbook with live formulas (openpyxl), then verify it against Python.

Conventions enforced by construction and re-checked by src/audit_excel.py:
  * Blue font = input (only on the Inputs tab; every input has a source and a date).
  * Black font = formula. No numeric literals inside formulas except 0 and 1 (structure).
  * Grey italic on light-grey fill = STATIC value produced by Python and pasted as a value
    (solver outputs, Monte Carlo, the CAGR x margin grid). Each such sheet says
    "static, generated by <module>".
  * The Forecast/Reverse blocks are produced by ONE function, so the base case and the three
    reverse checks use identical formulas.

Excel cannot run Goal Seek from openpyxl, so the implied CAGR / margin / WACC are stored as static
inputs (Inputs section 8) and the Reverse tab re-runs the full DCF with them as a live check.
"""
